> <b>[[Private Property]] Talk</b>
> 
> 040120, JunkerIn:
> 
> My beginning write-up--to be finished
> 
> First private property is things, i.e., any thing in the physical universe including human bodies. Not only is it a thing, but a thing which somebody has the right to use or control and thus also to dispose of, i.e., the disposal is a type of use, final, but still a use. And the human body is where the argumentation starts.
> 
> ---
> 
> As per Murry N. Rothbard, the starting question is then: Who owns the human body? Is the answer A) nobody, B) the occupant, C) someone or a group of someones else, or D) everyone? He notes that there are no further choices and considers each possible answer in detail. So too in this presentation.
> 
> A. Nobody is not a real answer, because someone (b) does control the body by default.
> 
> C. If someone else or a group is to control the body, there is instantly slavery. Slavery is considered immoral in itself, but more decisively, under implementation, it breaks the ZAP since the other can only 'control' the body through force or the threat of force should the occupant any any time disagree with that use or disposal.
> 
> D. Everyone cannot be polled for a decision. The body dies from starvation while waiting for the decision on whether or not to eat. Even if the world were small enough to poll everyone, each would be waiting for the others' approval in order to signal approval or disapproval, while they all in turn are waiting for everyone else's approval.
> 
> In the end, despite any moral judgements one way or the other, the argumentative answer is B: The occupant owns his own body and rightfully controls it and decides its disposal, and no one else has a claim to interfere.
> 
> 
> Next comes the argumentation about the other things in the environment.
> ----
