> = Money Introduction =
> 
> Money is not a thing, but rather a particular use of things. Anything that you trade for predominantly to use it in turn for further trade is being used as money. Many different things have been used as money at different times-- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, cattle, and of course, silver and gold.
> 
> There are certain attributes that allow more convenient use of certain things as money.
> 
> #Recognizability -- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, cattle, silver, and gold
> #Divisibility -- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, silver, and gold
> #Chemical/physical Stability -- shells, silver, and gold
> #High value to weight ratio -- silver, and gold
> 
> Historically, advanced or mature trade settled on silver and gold as the main items used as money mainly because it well meets all four attributes.
> 
> Then came strong, central governments and fiat monies. Gold and silver were used as money throughout the world until about World War One. WW1 bankrupted many governments, disrupted many businesses, and put the 'winners' in charge of the 'losers'. This resulted in many governments passing 'legal tender' laws and no longer allowing conversion of their printed paper bills into gold or silver. The major exception to this was the United States. Gold there was used by people as money until 1933, by government until 1974, and silver was used by people as money until 1964. 
> 
> And then... to be continued.
